From: Kevin Wang <wy721@qq.com>
Subject: [PATCH] x86/sev: expose Secure TSC status in sysfs

Userspace has no way to tell whether an SEV-SNP guest runs with Secure
TSC: CPUID only says the CPU supports it, and the SEV_STATUS MSR needs
the msr driver.  Add /sys/devices/system/cpu/sev/secure_tsc next to
vmpl, so dstack can refuse secure_time when the hypervisor still
controls the guest TSC.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [dstack specific]
Signed-off-by: Kevin Wang <wy721@qq.com>
---
--- a/arch/x86/coco/sev/core.c
+++ b/arch/x86/coco/sev/core.c
@@ -1431,10 +1431,19 @@ static ssize_t vmpl_show(struct kobject
 	return sysfs_emit(buf, "%d\n", snp_vmpl);
 }
 
+static ssize_t secure_tsc_show(struct kobject *kobj,
+			       struct kobj_attribute *attr, char *buf)
+{
+	return sysfs_emit(buf, "%d\n",
+			  cc_platform_has(CC_ATTR_GUEST_SNP_SECURE_TSC));
+}
+
 static struct kobj_attribute vmpl_attr = __ATTR_RO(vmpl);
+static struct kobj_attribute secure_tsc_attr = __ATTR_RO(secure_tsc);
 
 static struct attribute *vmpl_attrs[] = {
 	&vmpl_attr.attr,
+	&secure_tsc_attr.attr,
 	NULL
 };
 
